  "title": "Concussion: The Invisible Injury",
  "emoji": "🩺",
  "color": "#5B21B6",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply Concussion: The Invisible Injury.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A soccer player heads the ball, feels dizzy, and continues playing. The brain inside just bounced off the skull.",
      "visual": "⚽"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "A concussion is a mild traumatic brain injury that disrupts brain function without visible structural damage.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The impact stretches axons, disrupts ion flow, and triggers a metabolic crisis in affected neurons.",
      "visual": "⚡"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "CT and MRI scans often look normal after concussion—the damage is chemical and microscopic.",
      "visual": "🩻"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Repeated concussions cause chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE)—a degenerative disease found in many contact sport athletes.",
      "visual": "🏈"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "A concussion is never 'just a bump.' It's a brain injury that demands rest and time.",
      "visual": "🛑"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Why do concussions often not show up on brain scans?",
    "options": [
      "The damage is chemical and microscopic",
      "Concussions don't actually affect the brain",
      "Scans can only detect blood clots"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
